    'cybersecurity-analyst',
    'Cybersecurity Analyst',
    'Cybersecurity',
    'Cybersecurity Analysts protect systems, users and data from attacks, fraud and digital risk.',
    $career$<h2>What does a Cybersecurity Analyst do?</h2><p>A Cybersecurity Analyst monitors threats, investigates incidents, improves security controls and helps organisations reduce digital risk. The role is highly relevant as payments, cloud systems, student data, hospital records and enterprise apps move online.</p><h2>Best-fit students</h2><p>This career suits students who enjoy networks, operating systems, ethical hacking, investigation and structured thinking. Good starting routes include <a href="/courses/btech">B.Tech</a>, <a href="/courses/bca">BCA</a> and <a href="/courses/mca">MCA</a>.</p><h2>Skills to build first</h2><p>Focus on networking, Linux, security operations, SIEM tools, web security, identity management, scripting and incident response. Ethical practice matters because this career works with sensitive systems.</p><h2>Career outlook</h2><p>Demand is strong in banks, IT services, cloud companies, government vendors, ecommerce and consulting. Entry roles include SOC analyst, security analyst, vulnerability analyst and GRC analyst.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is cybersecurity only for hackers?</h3><p>No. It includes monitoring, compliance, cloud security, privacy, risk, audits and incident response.</p><h3>Which skill should I learn first?</h3><p>Networking and Linux are the best foundations before advanced tools.</p>$career$,
