  (
    'product-manager',
    'Product Manager',
    'Product and Business',
    'Product Managers decide what to build, why it matters and how teams should measure success.',
    $career$<h2>What does a Product Manager do?</h2><p>A Product Manager connects users, business goals, design, technology and data. The role is popular because digital companies need people who can turn customer problems into useful products and measurable outcomes.</p><h2>Best-fit students</h2><p>This career suits students who like strategy, communication, research and structured decision-making. Good routes include <a href="/courses/btech">B.Tech</a>, <a href="/courses/bba">BBA</a>, <a href="/courses/mba">MBA</a> and analytics-led programmes.</p><h2>Skills to build first</h2><p>Focus on user research, product analytics, prioritisation, writing, design thinking, stakeholder management and business metrics. Build case studies that show how you think.</p><h2>Career outlook</h2><p>Product roles are common in SaaS, fintech, consumer apps, edtech, ecommerce and AI products. Freshers often enter through associate product manager, business analyst or growth roles.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is coding required for product management?</h3><p>Coding is not mandatory, but technical understanding helps you work better with engineering teams.</p><h3>Is MBA required?</h3><p>No, but an MBA can help for business-heavy PM roles.</p>$career$,
